WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.250
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:08.760
There are many research projects about teaching
an AI to play video games well.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:14.070
We have seen some amazing results from DeepMind's
Deep Q Learning algorithm that performed on

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:17.789
a superhuman level on many games, but faltered
on others.

00:00:17.789 --> 00:00:23.130
What really made the difference is the sparsity
of rewards and the lack of longer-term planning.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:27.529
What this means is that the more often we
see the score change on our screen, the faster

00:00:27.529 --> 00:00:31.409
we know how well we are doing and change our
strategy if needed.

00:00:31.409 --> 00:00:35.591
For instance, if we make a mistake in Atari
Breakout, we lose a life almost immediately,

00:00:35.591 --> 00:00:41.400
but in a strategy game, a bad decision may
come back to haunt us up to an hour after

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:42.510
committing it.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:46.680
So, what can we do to build an AI that can
deal with these cases?

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.170
So far when we have talked about extrinsic
rewards that come from the environment, for

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:57.280
instance, our score in a video game, and most
existing AIs are for all intents and purposes,

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.650
extrinsic score-maximizing machines.

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:05.299
And this work is about introducing an intrinsic
reward by endowing an AI with one of the most

00:01:05.299 --> 00:01:08.229
humanlike attributes: curiosity.

00:01:08.229 --> 00:01:12.499
But hold on right there, how can a machine
possibly become curious?

00:01:12.499 --> 00:01:17.649
Well, curiosity is defined by whatever mathematical
definition we attach to it.

00:01:17.649 --> 00:01:22.389
In this work, curiosity is defined as the
AI's ability to predict the results of its

00:01:22.389 --> 00:01:23.770
own actions.

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:28.780
This is big, because it gives the AI tools
to pre-emptively start learning skills that

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:32.420
don't seem useful now, but might be useful
in the future.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:38.090
In short, this AI is driven to explore, even
if it hasn't been told how well it is doing.

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:43.639
It will naturally start exploring levels in
Super Mario, even without seeing the score.

00:01:43.639 --> 00:01:49.270
And now comes the great part: this curiosity
really teaches the AI to learn new skills,

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:54.130
and when we drop it into a new, previously
unseen level, it will perform much better

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:55.909
than a non-curious one.

00:01:55.909 --> 00:02:00.520
When playing Doom, the legendary first-person
shooter game, it will also start exploring

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:04.979
the level and is able to rapidly solve hard
exploration tasks.

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:10.920
The comparisons reveal that an AI infused
with curiosity performs significantly better

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:16.570
on easier tasks, but the even cooler part
is that with curiosity, we can further increase

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:21.720
the difficulty of the games and the sparsity
of external rewards and can expect the agent

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:25.450
to do well, even when previous algorithms
failed.

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:29.480
This will be able to play much harder games
than previous works.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:34.180
And remember, games are only used to demonstrate
the concept here, this will be able to do

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:35.470
so much more.

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:36.470
Love it.

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:39.599
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

